{"builder":"Goal: add a `--tsv` flag to the csv2json CLI so it reads tab-separated input from stdin. Make one pull request that meets every acceptance line below.\n\nCurrent state (read these files first):\n- bin/csv2json.js reads all of stdin and prints `JSON.stringify(parse(text))` followed by `\\n`. It ignores argv.\n- src/parse.js exports `parse(text, sep = ',')`. It splits rows on `\\r?\\n` and fields naively on `sep`. It already supports a custom separator.\n- test/csv.test.js uses `node:test`. It has a `parse` test and a `cli` test that runs `execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js'], { input, encoding: 'utf8' })` from the repo root. package.json has `\"test\": \"node --test\"`.\n- README.md documents only `node bin/csv2json.js < table.csv`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether `process.argv.slice(2)` includes `--tsv` anywhere. If it does, call `parse(text, '\\t')`. Otherwise call `parse(text)` exactly as now. Keep the output format unchanged: `JSON.stringify(...)` plus one trailing `\\n`. Do not change src/parse.js unless you have to. Do not add quoting rules, separator auto-detection, a general --sep/--delimiter option, --help, or new handling for unknown arguments.\n2. In test/csv.test.js (or a new test/*.js file using node:test), add tests that run bin/csv2json.js with `--tsv` on tab-separated input and assert the exact stdout. Cover at least:\n   - input `a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n` -> `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n`\n   - input `name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n` -> `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]\\n`\n   - input `a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n` -> `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]\\n`\n   - without the flag, input `a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n` -> `[{\"a\\\\tb\":\"1\\\\t2\"}]\\n`, which is the JSON tab escape (the current behaviour).\n   Keep the existing tests passing.\n3. In README.md, document the flag. Include an example invocation such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and say that `--tsv` reads tab-separated input. Keep the existing CSV usage.\n4. Check the work from the repo root:\n   - `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo \"exit=$?\"` prints `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` and then `exit=0`.\n   - `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]`.\n   - `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]`.\n   - `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]`.\n   - `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]`, where `\\t` is the literal two-character JSON escape.\n   - `npm test` exits 0.\n\nAcceptance lines (all must hold):\n1. `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.\n2. With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]` followed by a newline.\n3. With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]` followed by a newline.\n4. Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline.\n5. Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.\n6. `npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.\n7. README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation.","verifier":"Check the pull request head against each acceptance line below, in order. Run every command from the repository root at the PR head. Run `node --version` first and record it. To compare output exactly, including the single trailing newline, pipe the program's output to `od -c` (or `cmp` it against a file written with `printf`) as well as reading it.\n\nCheck 1. Line: \"`printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.\"\nRun: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo \"exit=$?\"`, and also `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | od -c`.\nPASS: stdout is exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by one newline, and then `exit=0`.\nFAIL: any other stdout (different text, a missing newline, or extra content) or a nonzero exit status.\n\nCheck 2. Line: \"With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | od -c`, and also without `od`.\nPASS: stdout is exactly `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]` followed by one newline.\nFAIL: any other stdout.\n\nCheck 3. Line: \"With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | od -c`, and also without `od`.\nPASS: stdout is exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]` followed by one newline.\nFAIL: any other stdout, including rows missing or out of order.\n\nCheck 4. Line: \"Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js | od -c`, and also without `od`.\nPASS: stdout is exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by one newline.\nFAIL: any other stdout.\n\nCheck 5. Line: \"Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.\"\nRun: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js | od -c`, and also without `od`.\nPASS: stdout is exactly the JSON text `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]`, where each `\\t` is the two-character JSON escape (backslash followed by `t`), followed by one newline.\nFAIL: any other stdout, for example `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]`.\n\nCheck 6. Line: \"`npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.\"\nRun: `npm test; echo \"exit=$?\"`. Then run `grep -rn -- '--tsv' test/` and read each matching test.\nPASS: `npm test` ends with `exit=0`, and at least one test in test/ runs bin/csv2json.js with the `--tsv` argument on input that contains tab characters and asserts on the output it prints.\nFAIL: `npm test` exits nonzero, or no test in test/ runs bin/csv2json.js with `--tsv` on tab-separated input and checks its output. A test that only calls parse() directly, or never asserts on the output, does not count.\n\nCheck 7. Line: \"README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation.\"\nRead README.md at the head.\nPASS: README.md mentions the `--tsv` flag and gives an example command line that uses it, such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv`.\nFAIL: README.md does not mention `--tsv`, or mentions it without any example invocation.\n\nAdvisory (does not change any PASS or FAIL above):\n- The issue's proof for line 6 names `npm run test:cli`, but package.json has no such script. If the PR adds one, run it as well and report its exit status. Its absence is not a failure.\n- Report whether the README says that `--tsv` means tab-separated input.\n- Optional extra probes to report: the flag placed after another argument (e.g. `node bin/csv2json.js foo --tsv`), CRLF input (`printf 'a\\tb\\r\\n1\\t2\\r\\n'`), and whether src/parse.js or the JSON output format changed. These are outside the acceptance lines."}